 - **The metric is reference-based.** CIDEr-D rewards matching the references' wording. In Vietnamese, the stripped column removes the trailing-period effect, and the PTB column agrees with it. Still, part of any `ret` − `rand` gain may be closer wording of image-specific content rather than better content. The study has no human judgments; by design, it uses existing data and labels only.
 - **Object measures depend on the matcher and the gold.** On OpenViIC, gold objects come from the union of the references, so a correct object that no reference names counts as an error. On XM3600, the verified labels cover only the mapped classes. The English arm registered no object-precision prediction.
 - **One test look, one machine, one run, one random draw.** Each test set was decoded once with greedy decoding. The CIs capture image sampling conditional on one seeded `rand` assignment per image and on full-set document frequencies; they do not include the variation from drawing other random examples, or run-to-run variation. Decode-parity checks that re-decoded the same development images in the same batch slices on the same software stack gave identical captions (52/52 per backbone; supplement, "Test-run operations", and Table S14); they show run-to-run parity, not independence from batch composition; a later development check found that decoding one image at a time changed some captions (Section 6.10.2).
-- **The attribution study is development-only, on re-used sets.** Its rows come from OV-dev-A, OV-dev-B and XM-dev500, which had carried earlier development rows; on OV-dev-A and OV-dev-B τ_sel was known, so τ_pair and τ_marg there are a split of a known sum. Whatever the outcome of its one look (Section 6.10.3), it is a development result for the backbones and configurations of that look, not a test result, and it says nothing about other sets, models or memories. Its registered claim can involve only Qwen2.5-VL-7B and InternVL3-8B, which share the Qwen2.5 language-model line; Aya-Vision-8B's rows are a first look with no claim. The copy bound rests on assumption A_cp, which can only overstate copying, and it bounds the stored batched contrast, not the batch-size-1 one.
+- **The attribution study is development-only, on re-used sets.** Its rows come from OV-dev-A, OV-dev-B and XM-dev500, which had carried earlier development rows; on OV-dev-A and OV-dev-B τ_sel was known, so τ_pair and τ_marg there are a split of a known sum. Whatever the outcome of its one look (Section 6.10.3), it is a development result for the backbones and configurations of that look, not a test result, and it says nothing about other sets, models or memories. Its registered claim can involve only Qwen2.5-VL-7B and InternVL3-8B, which share the Qwen2.5 language-model line; Aya-Vision-8B's rows are a first look with no claim. The copy bound rests on assumption A_cp, which can only overstate copying, and it bounds the stored batched contrast, not the batch-size-1 one. The registered positive control (Section 6.10.8) shows that the analysis returns DETECTED when a large retrieval-specific effect is present by construction, for an image-blind generator; it does not show sensitivity near +2.0, it has no negative control, and it does not re-validate the rows of the three backbones.
 - **Batch composition changes greedy captions.** For all three backbones, decoding the same images one at a time instead of in batches changed 8, 13 and 19 of 50 greedy captions (Section 6.10.2). The registered results of this paper are therefore results of the systems as run: fixed batches for both arms of every contrast between text-example arms of one backbone, and one query at a time for the LMCap re-implementation and the interleaved image-caption arm (Section 6.10.2). No number changes; but a per-image difference between arms decoded in batches can contain a batch-mate term, and the image bootstrap does not model the coupling of images within a batch slice. The attribution study's rows avoid this by decoding at batch size 1; the check is by string identity and says nothing about which policy gives better captions.
 - **The backbones' training data are not fully disclosed.** The Qwen2.5-VL report [@Bai2025Qwen25VL] describes its training data by category (image captions, interleaved image-text, OCR data that include Vietnamese); in our reading it does not name COCO, NoCaps or a Vietnamese caption set. The InternVL3 report [@Zhu2025InternVL3] builds on the corpora of InternVL2.5, whose report lists COCO captions, Open Images captions and TextCaps among its training data [@Chen2024InternVL25]. COCO images and captions, and Open Images images like those of NoCaps, may therefore have been seen in training, at least by InternVL3. The equal-k `rand` control removes contamination that `ret` and `rand` share, but not contamination that interacts with retrieval (retrieved captions cueing memorised references). "Training-free" means no task training in this study; this is a desk check of public reports, not an audit.
 - **The Flickr30K comparison is a baseline comparison, not a retrieval test.** The two Flickr30K `ret` − `rand` claims (E1, E2) passed the rehearsal without the registered margin and were not tested, so retrieved against random examples on Flickr30K rests on development rows only. The three E3 test claims compare each backbone with LMCap's 2.9B text-only generator, so exposure to Flickr30K or COCO in pre-training may differ between the two sides, and LMCap is a re-implementation with four disclosed deviations (Section 3.7), not the published system. The decision statistic sat at its floor for every claim, so it bounds the evidence by the resampling budget rather than measuring a tail probability, and the +2.0 SESOI was not recalibrated for PTB CIDEr-D (Section 8.6).
